 ],
 "literal": "As bees swarm, densely blossomed clusters on the branches appearing at intervals, / like crafted jewels made of gold worn by women / appearing in their hair, the fresh flowers of the Konrai, / even if the forest says it is the rainy season, / as for me, I do not believe it; he is not one who speaks lies.",
 "translation": "The fresh blossoms of the konrai tree, appearing at intervals in dense clusters where bees swarm, look like the crafted golden jewels worn by women in their dark hair. Even if the forest declares that the rainy season has arrived, I will not believe it; for my lover would never utter a falsehood.",
 "modern": "வண்டுகள் மொய்க்குமாறு நெருங்கி மலர்ந்த கிளைகளின் பூங்கொத்துகள் இடைவெளிவிட்டுத் தோன்றுவது, பொன்னால் செய்த அணிகலன்களை அணிந்த பெண்களின் கூந்தலில் விளங்குவது போலத் தோன்றும். புதிய மலர்களைக் கொண்ட கொன்றை மரங்கள் நிறைந்த இக்காடு 'கார்காலம் வந்துவிட்டது' என்று கூறினாலும், நான் அதனை நம்பமாட்டேன். ஏனெனில், என் தலைவர் பொய் சொல்பவர் அல்லர்.",
 "explanation": "This poem belongs to the Mullai (forest) landscape, which is associated with the theme of patient waiting during the rainy season. The heroine's lover had promised to return before the onset of the rains. Now, the Konrai (cassia) trees have burst into golden blossoms, which traditionally signals the arrival of the rainy season. However, the heroine refuses to accept that the season has truly begun because her lover has not yet returned. She reasons that since her lover is a man of truth who would never break his promise, the forest must be mistaken or lying, rather than her lover being late. The golden blossoms are beautifully compared to golden ornaments worn in the dark hair of women.",
